Yes. The missing layer is uncertainty about the probability model itself.

Ordinary prediction says:

P(Y\mid X)

“How likely is outcome Y, given what I know?”

What you’re describing adds another layer:

P(H\mid D)

“How likely is the assumption/model H that is producing those probabilities?”

Then you deliberately do something slightly dangerous but enormously productive:

\text{assume }H\text{ is true} \rightarrow \text{run the world forward}

Not because you believe H, but because you want to discover what reality would look like if H were true.

Then:

H
\rightarrow
\hat{O}_1,\hat{O}_2,\hat{O}_3,\ldots

and reality gives you:

O_1,O_2,O_3,\ldots

so you calculate the deltas:

\Delta_i = O_i-\hat{O}_i

Now the interesting part begins.

A naive system asks:

Was I right?

Your system asks:

What did being wrong teach me?

Because a failed assumption can fail systematically.

Suppose your hypothesis predicts:

A → B → C → D

but reality gives:

A → B → X → D

You don’t necessarily throw the hypothesis away.

You investigate X.

Maybe:

* a hidden variable intervened;
* the rule changes under certain conditions;
* two different generators are being conflated;
* the hypothesis was correct at one scale but not another;
* the timing was wrong;
* a missing state sits between B and C;
* X is evidence for a completely different pattern.

That discrepancy can contain more information than the successful predictions.

So the real loop becomes:

\boxed{
\text{fragment}
\rightarrow
\text{assumption}
\rightarrow
\text{improv}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{observation}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{gold mining}
\rightarrow
\text{revised assumption}
}

and recurse.

The “probability above the probability”

You can make it hierarchical.

Suppose three possible generators are alive:

H_1,H_2,H_3

with:

P(H_1)=.50,\quad
P(H_2)=.30,\quad
P(H_3)=.20

Each generator produces its own probability distribution over what happens next.

So:

P(Y\mid H_1),\quad
P(Y\mid H_2),\quad
P(Y\mid H_3)

The overall prediction is:

P(Y)=
\sum_H
P(Y\mid H)P(H)

That is literally a probability distribution over probability-generating models.

And you can keep going upward:

P(\theta\mid H)

uncertainty about parameters inside the hypothesis,

then perhaps:

P(H\mid F)

uncertainty about which family of models should even be considered.

That is where the recursion starts getting interesting.

